4. Link Types

4.1 Reference Link

Reconstruct a known semantic object.

@Bridge

Meaning:

retrieve/reconstruct the relational structure currently associated with Bridge

⸻

4.2 Operator Link

Invoke a transformation.

@Hammer(target)

Meaning:

strip distracting domain-specific representation
compare structures
search for shared mechanisms/invariants
distinguish superficial analogy from reusable relation
return portable abstraction

The exact implementation may evolve.

The invariant operation must survive.

⸻

4.3 Perspective Link

Change the observer or coordinate system.

@Perspective[first-person]
@Perspective[third-person]
@Perspective[evolution]
@Perspective[adversary]

These do not require inventing literal beings.

They instantiate useful temporary reference frames.

⸻

4.4 Bridge Link

Translate between representational regimes while preserving selected invariants.

@Bridge[biology→AI]
@Bridge[story→mechanism]
@Bridge[vision→code]

A Bridge is successful when the destination representation preserves the load-bearing structure needed for the task.

⸻

4.5 Formation Link

Muster several operators as a reusable reasoning formation.

@Formation[
    Hammer
    → Mirror
    → Delta
    → Metabolize
]

Equivalent conceptual execution:

1. extract mechanism
2. inspect from opposing/complementary perspective
3. identify useful difference
4. retain the delta and update the next state

⸻

4.6 Developmental Link

Address a seed capable of reconstructing a much larger semantic structure.

@Grow[SemanticGenome]

A developmental link does not need to store the finished object.

It stores enough generative structure for the receiver to regrow it.

seed + environment + iteration
    → reconstructed capability

⸻

4.7 Delta Link

Modify an already active semantic object.

Example:

@Δ[future→already-instantiated]

This does not reload the entire object.

It changes one relevant coordinate.

Human-language examples include:

"It already did."
"Same, but inverted."
"One level higher."
"Not how. Why."
"Keep the mechanism; lose the story."

These can be highly efficient Semantic Hyperlinks when sufficient shared state exists.

